---
import LegalLayout from '../../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { getLegalPage, legalHref } from '../../lib/legal';
import { SITE } from '../../lib/seo';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';

const page = getLegalPage('copyright')!;
---

<LegalLayout
	page={page}
	intro="How ownership, attribution, and third-party rights work on Digital Catholic."
>
	<h2>1. Ownership of original Digital Catholic content</h2>
	<p>
		Unless otherwise stated, original prose written for Digital Catholic, the site’s compilation and
		arrangement of educational materials, and original branding elements (including the Digital Catholic
		name presentation and site design as published) are owned by the project maintainer / copyright
		holders identified in the repository licence files, subject to third-party rights noted below.
	</p>
	<p>
		The software that builds the Website is separately licensed under the MIT Licence described in
		<code>LICENSE.md</code> in the public repository. That software licence does not automatically place
		all article text, images, or third-party materials into the public domain or under MIT terms.
	</p>

	<h2>2. Copyright protection for original articles and design</h2>
	<p>
		Original articles, page copy, and the expressive design of Digital Catholic are protected by the
		Copyright Act 1968 (Cth) and, where applicable, the laws of other countries. Unauthorised commercial
		reproduction of substantial original content may infringe copyright, except where a statutory
		exception (such as fair dealing for criticism, review, or reporting) or an express licence applies.
	</p>

	<h2>3. Third-party images, illustrations, and artwork</h2>
	<p>
		Many illustrations are historical artworks used under public-domain status, Creative Commons licences,
		or other stated permissions. Digital Catholic does <strong>not</strong> claim ownership of third-party
		images. Captions, figure notes, and credit files (for example under <code>public/**/CREDITS.md</code>)
		identify sources where recorded. If an attribution is missing or incorrect, please report it.
	</p>

	<h2>4. Scripture translation copyright</h2>
	<p>
		Bible translations are typically protected by copyright held by publishers or rights organisations,
		even when individual verses are quoted for educational discussion. Digital Catholic does not claim
		ownership of any modern Scripture translation. Short quotations used for commentary should not be
		taken as a licence to reproduce an entire translation. Prefer official editions for liturgy and study.
	</p>

	<h2>5. Traditional and modern prayer attribution</h2>
	<p>
		Traditional prayers may be ancient or of uncertain authorship; modern prayers, translations, and
		devotional texts may remain under copyright. We do not assume that every prayer text on the internet
		is public domain. Where a specific modern text is used, attribution and licence terms should be
		respected. If you believe a prayer text is misattributed or used beyond its licence, contact us.
	</p>

	<h2>6. Public-domain content</h2>
	<p>
		Some materials (for example many older artworks, or texts whose copyright has expired) may be in the
		public domain in Australia or elsewhere. Public-domain status can differ by country and by the
		particular edition or translation. We do not assert that every religious text or Church-related
		document is public domain.
	</p>

	<h2>7. Creative Commons licensing</h2>
	<p>
		Where an image or text is used under a Creative Commons licence, the terms of that licence (including
		attribution, share-alike, or non-commercial conditions) continue to apply. Reusers should follow the
		licence named in the credit line or source page, not assume Digital Catholic can re-licence that work.
	</p>

	<h2>8. Attribution requirements</h2>
	<p>When quoting or republishing material from Digital Catholic, please:</p>
	<ul>
		<li>Identify Digital Catholic as the source of original site prose you reproduce.</li>
		<li>Link to the specific page URL where practicable.</li>
		<li>Retain third-party credit lines for images and licensed texts.</li>
		<li>Do not imply Church endorsement or authorship that does not exist.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>9. Reproduction and redistribution permissions</h2>
	<p>
		Non-commercial sharing of links, and short quotations with attribution for teaching or personal study,
		are welcome. For republication of substantial original Digital Catholic articles (for example in a
		book, paid course, or mirrored site), please request permission via
		<a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>
		before proceeding, unless a statutory exception clearly applies.
	</p>

	<h2>10. Copyright infringement reporting</h2>
	<p>
		If you believe content on Digital Catholic infringes your copyright or related rights, follow the
		procedure on
		<a href={legalHref('corrections')}>Content Corrections &amp; Copyright Requests</a>.
		Include identification of the work, the URL of the allegedly infringing material, your contact
		details, and a good-faith statement of your rights.
	</p>

	<h2>11. Content removal and correction</h2>
	<p>
		We may remove, revise, or replace content to correct errors, improve attribution, respond to valid
		rights complaints, or reflect updated Church or historical information. See also the
		<a href={legalHref('disclaimer')}>Religious &amp; Educational Disclaimer</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>12. Contact</h2>
	<p>
		Copyright questions:
		<a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>
		· <a href={withBase('/contact')}>Contact</a>
		· <a href={SITE.repoUrl} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub repository<span class="visually-hidden"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a>
	</p>
</LegalLayout>
